-- title:
--   Finite adaptive returns to a parent and their retained children
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals.
--
--   1. **Positive-support extraction.** For convex coefficients $\beta$ on $\{0..N-1\}$, there are $q\ge1$ and an order embedding $e:\{0..q-1\}\to\{0..N-1\}$ onto exactly the indices with $\beta_i>0$. The retained coefficients sum to $1$, and
--   $$
--   \sum_j\beta_{e(j)}u_{e(j)}=\sum_i\beta_iu_i .
--   $$
--   2. **Adaptive parent return.** A record of a finite legal run: states, selected candidates and convex coefficients $\beta$ whose average of evaluations is within $\tau$ of a target $x$. It also records the order embedding retaining exactly the positive coefficients, with the same average.
--   3. **Existence.** Such returns exist under the hypotheses of adaptive finite selection, and one is chosen.
